\section{Group-level standard deviations}{

\code{set_prior(class = "sd", group = "g", coef = "x")} puts the density
on one standard deviation: that of the \code{x} column of the \code{g} block.
brms puts the same prior on \code{sd_g__x}. The other standard deviations
of the block keep the prior of the group or of the class. A \code{coef}
without a \code{group} is refused, because brms has no such row.

The density is on the standard deviation itself, with the
log-Jacobian of the log map. frmtmb holds a correlated block as log
standard deviations and separate correlation parameters. It does not
hold a Cholesky factor. So each standard deviation is one parameter,
and the prior on it is exact. The prior does not change the
correlations. A class \code{"cor"} prior addresses those. In a Student-t
block (\code{gr(g, dist = "student")}), the parameter is the scale. That
is also what brms's \code{sd} is in that term.

Some structures give their columns one shared standard deviation,
for example \code{homdiag()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{homcs()}. They have no standard
deviation per coefficient. A \code{coef} that names one of their columns
is refused by name. Drop the \code{coef} to put the prior on the shared
standard deviation. A block of another response, distributional
parameter or nonlinear parameter does not cause this refusal.

The group labels are brms's. A nested \code{(1 | g1/g2)} has the groups
\code{g1} and \code{g1:g2}. A multi-membership \code{(1 | mm(m1, m2))} has the group
\code{mmm1m2}. \code{\link[=default_prior]{default_prior()}} lists them so.
}

\section{Where an intercept prior lands}{

brms centers its design matrix and constrains the intercept at the
MEAN of the predictors, recovering the reported one as
\code{b_Intercept = Intercept - dot_product(means_X, b)}. frmtmb
parameterizes by the intercept at zero and evaluates a class
\code{"Intercept"} density at \verb{b0 + means_X'b}, so the two packages
constrain the same quantity. The map between the two
parameterizations is unit triangular and carries no Jacobian.

This matters whenever a predictor is not centered. On
\code{Reaction ~ Days}, where \code{mean(Days)} is 4.5, the intercept at zero
is strongly correlated with the slope and the intercept at the mean
is orthogonal to it, so a prior on the first biases the slope and a
prior on the second does not. Earlier releases used the intercept
at zero, and \code{brms::get_prior()}'s own default there moved the slope
by 0.068 standard errors; it now moves it by 0.00003.

Every sub-formula with an intercept is centered separately, as brms
does (\code{means_X}, \code{means_X_sigma}, ...). A NONLINEAR parameter's
sub-formula is not centered on either side, and neither are a
smooth's unpenalized columns or a \code{mo()} term, which sit outside
brms's \code{Xc} as well. To put a density on the intercept at zero, name
it as a coefficient instead: \verb{class = "b", coef = "Intercept"}.
A formula written \code{0 + Intercept + x}, or \code{bf(center = FALSE)}, makes
that the intercept's only prior slot, as brms does: the intercept is
then class \code{"b"} and a class \code{"b"} prior without a coef reaches it
too, while class \code{"Intercept"} has no slot and is refused.
}

\section{Ordinal thresholds}{

\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()} and \code{acat()} have no intercept
column: the thresholds replace it. brms priors them as its
\code{Intercept} class and so does frmtmb, so
\code{set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "Intercept")} on an
ordinal model addresses the whole threshold vector. It addresses the
THRESHOLDS, at the mean of the predictors (at zero under
\code{bf(center = FALSE)}, as in brms), with the log-Jacobian of
the map from frmtmb's internal storage. \code{cumulative()} holds
\verb{(tau_1, log increments)}, which is the same map Stan's \code{ordered}
type applies to brms's ordered thresholds. \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()} and
\code{acat()} hold the thresholds themselves, which brms declares as an
unconstrained vector, so there is no Jacobian. \code{lb}/\code{ub} are refused
there, because one number cannot box a whole vector of thresholds.

One threshold is addressed by its number, as brms lists it:
\code{set_prior("normal(0, 1)", class = "Intercept", coef = "2")} is a
density on the second threshold, and it replaces a class-wide
density there and only there. The vector stays one entry, so the
log-Jacobian of \code{cumulative()}'s map enters once. Under
\code{thres(gr = )} a threshold is named by its level and its number,
\verb{group = "a", coef = "2"}. brms has no per-threshold parameter
under \code{threshold = "equidistant"}, and the row is refused there, as
in brms.

The threshold structure changes what class \code{"Intercept"} addresses,
as it does in brms. Under \code{threshold = "equidistant"} it is the
first threshold alone, at the mean of the predictors, and class
\code{"delta"} is the distance between thresholds: on \code{delta} itself,
with the log-Jacobian of the log that \code{cumulative()} and
\code{hurdle_cumulative()} hold it on, since brms bounds it below by 0
there. Under \code{threshold = "sum_to_zero"} class \code{"Intercept"} is
refused: brms's prior there is on thresholds it declares before
centering them, whose common location only that prior places, and
frmtmb estimates the centered thresholds directly, so the parameter
does not exist.
}

\section{Hard bounds}{

\code{lb}/\code{ub} are how a box constraint is written. A specification may
carry bounds alone (\code{prior = ""}), a distribution alone, or both. A
bounds-only specification for a slot another specification already
gives a density is refused as a duplicate, as in brms; a bound on a
coefficient beside a class-wide density is a different slot and
boxes that coefficient under the class density.

A bound is addressed exactly like the distribution beside it, so
\code{set_prior("", nlpar = "guess", lb = 0, ub = 1)} bounds the nonlinear
parameter \code{guess}, and \code{dpar}, \code{resp}, \code{group} and \code{coef} narrow a
bound the same way they narrow a density. As with a distribution,
class \code{"b"} with \code{nlpar} covers EVERY coefficient of that parameter;
\code{coef} picks out one.

The scale is the parameter's own: class \code{"sd"} bounds a standard
deviation on the sd scale (frmtmb stores its log), classes \code{"ar"},
\code{"ma"} and \code{"cosy"} bound the natural coefficient of a residual
structure, and class \code{"theta"} bounds an internal covariance
parameter itself, one position at a time with \code{coef = "theta_12"},
where no class reaches that parameter (see class \code{"theta"} above).
A correlation takes no bound, as in brms: a correlation matrix's
entries are not free of one another, and brms writes a Stan program
that does not parse for \code{lb} on class \code{"cor"}. Everything else is
bounded on the
internal (link) scale, so a bound on a log-linked dispersion is a
bound on its logarithm.

In \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}} a bound is a box constraint handed to the optimizer; in
\code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} it becomes one of Stan's constrained
transforms. Both take this spelling and no other: the \code{lower}/\code{upper}
arguments of releases before 0.49 are gone rather than aliased, and a
call still using them fails as an unused argument. Every outer
parameter they could reach has a class here, down to a single
internal covariance parameter.

Where the two spellings differed, this one broadcasts: a bound
carried by \verb{nlpar =} covers every coefficient of that parameter, the
way a prior does, and \code{coef} narrows it to one. When a class-wide
and a coefficient-specific specification both bound a parameter, the
coefficient-specific one applies.
}

\section{Residual correlation}{

frmtmb holds an \code{ar()}, \code{ma()}, \code{arma()}, \code{cosy()} or \code{unstr()}
residual block in one unconstrained vector, chosen so the optimizer
cannot step outside the stationary and invertible region. A prior is
still written about the parameter brms names, and carried onto that
vector with the log Jacobian of the map, exactly as class \code{"sd"}
carries a density on a standard deviation onto its logarithm. So
\code{set_prior("normal(0, 0.5)", class = "ar")} is a density on the AR
coefficient itself, and \code{summary()} reports the parameter the prior
was written about.

Bounds behave the same way where the map allows it. A first-order
\code{ar}, \code{ma} or \code{cosy} coefficient is a monotone function of one
internal parameter, so \code{lb}/\code{ub} map exactly onto a box. At order two
and above they do not: \code{ar[1]} is a function of every internal
parameter of the block at once, so no box in internal space is the
box asked for, and \code{lb}/\code{ub} are refused rather than approximated.
Little is lost, because the parameterization already guarantees
stationarity and invertibility, which is what such a bound is usually
for, and brms's \code{cov = TRUE} form is of order one only. Under
\code{cov = FALSE} the coefficients are unconstrained and are the internal
parameters, so \code{lb}/\code{ub} apply at any order, as in brms. A bound at
an end of the parameter's own interval, such as brms's \code{lb = -1} on a
first-order \code{ar}, bounds nothing and is accepted, so a
\code{\link[=default_prior]{default_prior()}} table can be passed back as \verb{prior =}.

\code{cosy} is bounded below at \code{-1/(d - 1)} for \code{d} time points, where a
compound-symmetric matrix stops being positive definite, and a bound
outside that window is refused rather than clamped. brms bounds
\code{cosy} on \verb{[0, 1]} instead, so a negative estimate here has no brms
counterpart. \code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} samples \code{cosy} on brms's
\verb{[0, 1]}: its default prior there is brms's flat one, and a \code{cosy}
prior that sets no \code{lb} keeps \code{lb = 0}, as brms keeps the bound it
declares. \code{default_prior(route = "sample")} lists that interval, and
the default route lists frm()'s window.

Under a first-order \code{cov = TRUE} \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} or \code{arma()}, frmtmb's
\code{sigma} (in \code{summary()}, \code{sigma()} and \code{VarCorr()}) is the MARGINAL
sd of a residual, and brms's \code{sigma} is the innovation sd, smaller by
\code{c = sqrt(1 - ar^2)}, \code{1 / sqrt(1 + ma^2)} or
\verb{sqrt((1 - ar^2) / (1 + ma^2 + 2 ar ma))}: the same model, another
name. A density on class \code{"sigma"} is read as brms reads it, at
\code{sigma * c}, with the Jacobian of \code{c}, so \code{set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "sigma")} is brms's prior on its sigma. A \code{lb} above 0
or a \code{ub} on \code{sigma} is refused there, because a box on the
innovation sd moves with \code{ar} and is no box on the marginal sd frmtmb
holds. Under \code{sigma ~ x} with such a term, a prior on the intercept of
\code{sigma} (\verb{class = "Intercept", dpar = "sigma"}) is still read at
frmtmb's log marginal sd, where brms reads it at the log innovation
sd; the slopes (\verb{class = "b", dpar = "sigma"}) are the same in both.
}

\section{The LKJ prior}{

\code{lkj(eta)} is the density \code{det(C)^(eta - 1)} over a block's
correlation matrix \code{C}, normalized: \code{eta = 1} is uniform over
correlation matrices, larger \code{eta} concentrates toward the identity.
frmtmb holds a correlation as an unconstrained row-normalized
Cholesky parameter rather than as \code{C}, so the density is carried onto
those parameters with the exact Jacobian of that map (the derivation
is in the source of \code{R/priors.R}; \code{tests/testthat/test-lkj.R} checks
the sampled correlations against the closed-form LKJ marginals). The
prior a FLAT correlation parameter carries instead is
\code{(1 - rho^2)^(-3/2)}, which is improper.

It fits \code{us()} and \code{gr(cov = )} blocks of two or more terms, which
hold a whole correlation matrix, and the one-parameter structures
\code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()}, whose single bounded correlation
takes the LKJ marginal \code{(1 - rho^2)^(eta - 1)} with that structure's
own Jacobian. A \code{cs()} correlation is bounded below at \code{-1/(d - 1)},
where a compound-symmetric matrix stops being positive definite, and
the density is renormalized over that window. \code{toep()} is refused:
its parameterization is not positive definite everywhere, so it has
no correlation matrix to put a density on.
}

\section{Nonlinear parameters}{

\code{nlpar} addresses one parameter of an \code{nl = TRUE} formula, brms's
spelling: \code{set_prior("normal(5000, 1000)", nlpar = "ult")}, or
\code{prior(normal(5000, 1000), nlpar = "ult")}. Class \code{"b"} there covers
EVERY coefficient of that parameter, its intercept included, because
a nonlinear parameter's sub-formula is not centered and brms holds
its intercept in the same coefficient vector as its slopes. That is
why the vignette spelling above lands on \code{ult_(Intercept)} rather
than on nothing. Narrow to one column with \code{coef} (\code{"Intercept"} and
\code{"(Intercept)"} both name the intercept), or write
\verb{class = "Intercept", nlpar = "ult"}, which frmtmb stores as that
same slot, class \code{"b"} with \code{coef = "Intercept"}, so the two
spellings are one slot; brms does not take the second. \code{nlpar} narrows
classes \code{"sd"} and \code{"cor"} to the random-effect blocks of that
parameter as well.

A class \code{"b"} or \code{"Intercept"} specification without \code{nlpar} does not
reach a nonlinear parameter. On a model whose location is nonlinear,
such a specification addresses no parameter, and it is refused, as
in brms. A distributional parameter keeps its own spelling there:
\code{set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "sigma")} needs no
\code{nlpar}.

A prior with a location places \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}'s \code{start} for a nonlinear
parameter. \code{normal()}, \code{student_t()} and \code{cauchy()} all carry one,
and where \code{start} does not set a nonlinear coefficient, that
coefficient begins at the prior's location, reported in a message.
Other parameters keep their usual starts: a prior is a penalty, not
a claim about where to begin. Without a located prior a nonlinear
model still needs \code{start}, because \code{frm()} evaluates the objective
AT the starting values; \code{\link[=par_template]{par_template()}} names them.

\code{resp} picks one response of a multivariate model, and a nonlinear
parameter of one response is addressed with both \code{resp} and
\code{nlpar}.
}

\section{Translating a brms prior}{

\code{frm(prior = )} takes a \code{brmsprior} object directly, whether it came
from \code{brms::set_prior()}, \code{brms::prior()} or \code{brms::get_prior()}. A
row applies whatever its \code{prior} string says, which is brms's own
rule; an empty string is brms's flat default and applies nothing.
The \code{source} column is not read. Earlier releases dropped rows marked
\code{source == "default"} were dropped, which lost a prior the user had
edited into a \code{get_prior()} table in place, because brms does not
update \code{source} after that edit.

\strong{A specification means here what the same words mean in brms.}
Every class brms writes that frmtmb can honor is a class
\code{set_prior()} takes under the same name and with the same meaning,
so a table and a hand-written specification reach one code path:
\itemize{
\item \code{b}, \code{Intercept}, \code{sd}, \code{cor}, \code{ar}, \code{ma}, \code{cosy}, \code{cortime} and
\code{rescor} are frmtmb's own class names and keep their meaning.
\item a DISTRIBUTIONAL parameter's own class (\code{sigma}, \code{shape}, \code{phi},
\code{nu}, \code{kappa}, \code{sigma1}, ...) is a density on that parameter
ITSELF, through the dpar's inverse link with that map's
log-Jacobian, which is the change of variables class \code{"sd"}
performs. A bound travels with it, so \code{lb = 0} on a log-linked
dispersion becomes no constraint rather than a floor of 1.
\item \code{simo} and \code{sbhaz} keep their meaning too: a \code{dirichlet()} on a
\code{mo()} simplex and on a \code{cox()} baseline.
\item \code{theta}/\code{theta1}/\code{theta2}, \code{sds}, \code{sdgp}, \code{lscale},
\code{sdcar}, \code{car}, \code{meanme}, \code{sdme} and \code{corme} are refused by name,
each saying where frmtmb keeps that quantity instead, or that it
has no prior slot. A refusal is deliberate: translating
one of them would produce a different model rather than no model.
\item a \code{coef} on a \code{sd} or \code{cor} row is refused for the same reason.
brms narrows such a row to one coefficient of a block; frmtmb
resolves those classes per BLOCK, so applying the row without its
\code{coef} would put the density on every standard deviation of the
block, which is a wider prior than the one written. \code{coef} on
class \code{"b"} narrows as it does in brms and is unaffected.
}

Every refused row of a table is named in ONE message, because a table
is edited as a whole and stopping at the first bad row costs a round
trip per bad row.

brms's \code{tag} and \code{check} have no counterpart: \code{tag} names a prior
for reuse inside a Stan program, and \code{check} passes an unchecked
string through to one. frmtmb compiles no Stan program, so both are
omitted rather than accepted and ignored.
}

\section{A distributional parameter's own class}{

A distributional parameter has TWO spellings, and which one applies
is decided by the model rather than by taste. They are brms's own
two, and brms accepts each only on the model the other does not:

\describe{
\item{\code{class = "sigma"}}{a density on sigma ITSELF, on its natural
scale. Available when sigma has no predictor of its own, which
is the model where sigma is a single number.}
\item{\verb{class = "Intercept", dpar = "sigma"}}{a density on the
LINK-scale intercept of sigma's linear predictor. Available when
the model gives sigma a formula, \code{sigma ~ 1} included.}
}

Writing a formula for a parameter is what replaces the parameter
with a linear predictor, so \code{bf(y ~ x, sigma ~ 1)} has no \code{sigma} to
put a density on, and \code{bf(y ~ x)} has no \code{Intercept_sigma}. Each
spelling is refused by name on the other's model, naming the one
that applies, because a silently retargeted prior is a different
model rather than no model.

\verb{class = "b", dpar = "sigma"} addresses that predictor's SLOPES and
is on the link scale, in both packages. It needs slopes to exist: on
\code{sigma ~ 1} the predictor is an intercept only, so the row addresses
nothing, and it is refused rather than accepted as a silent no-op.
brms refuses it there too.

A distributional class names ONE parameter, so it takes \code{resp} and
neither \code{coef} nor \code{group}; both are refused rather than dropped.
On a multivariate model it needs \code{resp}, as it does in brms: without
it, the specification is refused.

\code{\link[=get_prior]{get_prior()}} lists whichever of the two spellings a model offers.
Where a parameter has NEITHER, because frmtmb refuses its class by
name, the table leaves it out rather than advertising a slot nothing
can fill: a mixture proportion with no predictor of its own is one,
and \code{?set_prior}'s refusal says where that quantity lives.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(100), z = rnorm(100),
                 g = factor(rep(1:10, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(100, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(10, 0, 0.8)[dd$g], 1)

# `+` combines specifications; the class-wide one goes first so the
# coefficient-specific one can override it
pr <- set_prior("normal(0, 1)", class = "b") +
  set_prior("normal(0, 0.2)", class = "b", coef = "z") +
  set_prior("exponential(1)", class = "sd", group = "g")
pr

# the priors penalize the likelihood: the fit is a MAP estimate
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd, prior = pr)
fixef_by_dpar(fit)$mu
# the tight prior on z shrinks it toward zero
fixef_by_dpar(frm(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd))$mu

# an empty distribution string sets a hard bound only
set_prior("", class = "b", coef = "x", lb = 0)

# a distributional parameter's own class is a density on the
# parameter itself, on its own scale, where the model gives that
# parameter no predictor. This model does not, so this is the
# spelling it offers, and get_prior() lists it
fit_s <- frm(bf(y ~ x + z) + gaussian(), data = dd,
             prior = set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)",
                               class = "sigma"))
sigma(fit_s)
# with sigma ~ 1 the model has a log-scale intercept instead, and
# that is the slot the prior addresses. brms draws the same line
set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "Intercept",
          dpar = "sigma")

# bounds address a nonlinear parameter the way a distribution does,
# so a guessing rate is held in [0, 1]
set_prior("", nlpar = "guess", lb = 0, ub = 1)

# the residual-correlation classes are brms's own names
set_prior("normal(0, 0.5)", class = "ar")
set_prior("lkj(2)", class = "rescor")
# and one internal covariance parameter that no class names, such
# as a toep() correlation, by its name as get_prior() lists it
set_prior("", class = "theta", coef = "theta_12", lb = -2, ub = 2)

# class "cor" addresses a correlated block as a whole, brms's
# spelling; eta > 1 pulls the correlation toward zero
dd$z <- rnorm(100)
dd$y2 <- dd$y + rnorm(10, 0, 0.6)[dd$g] * dd$z
fitc <- frm(bf(y2 ~ x + z + (z | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd,
            prior = set_prior("lkj(4)", class = "cor"))
VarCorr(fitc)

# get_prior() shows which rows a design offers
get_prior(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)

# prior() quotes its first argument, brms's spelling, and reaches
# the same machinery
prior(normal(0, 1), class = "b")
}
